data: {"sid":"04a23c5d-d305-4388-9869-a2a957c00c6f","m":{"id":"ec659e53-16be-4e87-9ce3-f2348d672ad2","role":"assistant","kind":"tool_use","text":"Write: {\n  \"file_path\": \"/Users/eugenechan/dev/personal/fiftyworkout/improvement-log/improvement-log-20261015-032330.md\",\n  \"content\": \"# Improvement Log — Session 20261015-032330\\n\\n## Tracker\\n\\n- [ ] 2026-10-16 — flowdeck-guard hook blocks any Bash command whose text contains \\\"open\\\" followed by a `.app` domain, even inside prose\\n\\n## Log\\n\\n### 2026-10-16 — flowdeck-guard false positive on prose\\n\\n**What happened:** Two Bash commands that wrote markdown were blocked with \\\"BLOCKED: open <app>.app\\\". The text contained the words \\\"open\\\" and \\\"fiftystrong.app\\\" on one line (in a heredoc, and in a Python string).\\n**Why it matters:** The hook scans the whole command string, so any prose with \\\"open\\\" before a `.app` domain fails. fiftystrong.app is the house domain, so this will keep happening.\\n**What better looks like:** Anchor the hook's match to a command position (start of the command, or after `;`, `&&`, `|`), not anywhere in the string. Workaround: write the text with the Write tool, then append it with `cat >>`.\\n\"\n}","ts":1792138401459,"toolUseId":"toolu_01VfiZjqFFo1Ui6usaHqzWkj","tool":{"name":"Write","target":"/Users/eugenechan/dev/personal/fiftyworkout/improvement-log/improvement-log-20261015-032330.md"}}}

